export const sourceMarkup = "<div class=\"eyebrow\">The foundation of the lab</div><h2>Real sources. Clear boundaries.</h2><p>The six lessons connect electric potential, the heartbeat, lead perspectives, interval measurement, clinical examples and the Sandbox. The anatomy, teaching signals and clinical recordings have different sources and limits, which remain visible throughout.</p><p>Modules 2, 3, 5 and 6 offer 0.03×, 0.1×, 0.3× and 1× playback. Module 1 retains the imported Cardiac Voltage experience’s own chapter controls and scientific notes. The twelve-lead sheet uses compact groups with consecutive time windows and one global cursor; its long rhythm strips cover the whole sweep. Clinical examples appear in a responsive multirow grid, and their traces can be clicked to seek and pause. Module 4 stays stationary for measurement.</p><h3>What is simulated?</h3><p>The reference sinus rhythm in Modules 2–4 is an original, idealized model. At 72 bpm it has PR 160 ms, QRS 90 ms and QT 380 ms. All wave markers and interval values use the same timing definitions. Limb leads satisfy the voltage identities exactly. Chest-lead morphology and regional terms are illustrative, not a forward calculation from the anatomical mesh.</p><p>In Modules 2, 3, 5 and 6, one shared deformation field moves the registered heart surfaces, vessels and attached pathways together. The ventricular cavities narrow as the walls thicken and twist; the outer envelope moves less than the inner walls. The valve plane descends toward a relatively stable apex. Distant great-vessel trunks remain nearly fixed, with flexible roots and subtle pulsation. Atrial contraction and reservoir expansion have separate timing. Contraction follows electrical activation. These kinematics are informed by cardiac mechanics; no patient motion, strain or ejection fraction is inferred from the ECG. The surface and cutaway now evaluate a continuous local action potential from a chamber-wise transfer of the Module 1 CT reference AT/APD fields. Finite endocardial-to-epicardial delay, local AP duration, and the imported normalized human optical recovery template determine the colors. The assumed absolute voltage scale and upstroke are not patient measurements. This coordinate transfer is not a patient registration, and the ordinary teaching traces are still not a forward calculation from this display. The ventricular wall is a combined reference surface. Omitting a contribution is a mathematical demonstration, not ablation or a disease simulation.</p><p>The electrical cutaway sections the actual myocardial surfaces, preserving chamber cavities, septum and cut wall faces. It shares the surface model’s contraction. Teal fibers and moving pulses show a registered teaching conduction network; deep red surface vessels are the coronaries. The x–y reference plane stays in the patient’s frontal frame. Only electrodes needed by selected leads and their references are displayed.</p><div class=\"source-item\"><b>New electrical views across Modules 2–6</b><p>The registered reference field has five views: membrane voltage, derivative-based moving fronts, activation time, recovery time, and action-potential duration. The surface, section and working-muscle AP rows share one evaluator. Static maps are neutral where organized timing is unavailable, including qualitative AF/flutter and ventricular tachyarrhythmia illustrations. Module 4 retains its stationary measurement desk and adds a frozen electrical snapshot.</p><p><a href=\"./data/electrical-atlas.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Field provenance and assumptions ↗</a>. The recovery atlas is an educational coordinate transfer between reference anatomies. Teaching activation now follows the same connected pathway and muscle-junction model as the visible impulses, with surface-geodesic spread and a separate outward wall delay. This is not a patient registration or a coupled ionic tissue simulation. Local recovery remains AT + APD; local APD is adjusted to preserve the reference recovery pattern.</p><p><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16606830/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Human activation and recovery imaging (2006) ↗</a> and <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11226755/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">normal and bundle-block ECGI maps (2024) ↗</a> support local recovery timing and demonstrate substantial variation between people. These observations inform the hypotheses; they do not supply the patients’ missing activation maps.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Fitted propagation · complete RBBB and anterior STEMI</b><p>The fit explorer uses a separate native CT source mesh and a physical torso lead-field forward calculation. Source physiology, common effective source gains, and bounded heart-pose assumptions are optimized against calibrated beat ensembles from PTB-XL 03155 and ACS00004. Original samples stay available. The same fitted source parameters produce the displayed local voltage and calculated ECG; camera movement does not change the signal.</p><p>Native 500 Hz beats share one R alignment. A single constant PR reference is removed from each lead in each beat; no added high-pass filter or separate P/QRS/T time warp is applied. Pointwise medians and beat IQR show persistent morphology and variation. Alternating accepted beats are held out from fitting. This tests within-record repeatability, not a new patient or invasive activation truth.</p><p>The fit is one constrained explanation on generic anatomy. Source patterns, geometry and effective gains are not uniquely identifiable from a surface ECG. Residuals, phase errors, baseline sensitivity, bound-active parameters and numerical checks remain visible. The inferred injury region is not a measured infarct boundary; the resting-versus-plateau split is an imposed prior.</p><p><a href=\"./data/pathology-fits/FORWARD_FIT_METHODS.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fitting experiments and results ↗</a> · <a href=\"./data/pathology-fits/TARGET_METHODS.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beat preparation, sources and licenses ↗</a> · <a href=\"./data/pathology-fits/manifest.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Published fit index ↗</a>.</p><p>Primary methods: <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9539798/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gillette et al., whole-heart electrophysiology and BBB</a>; <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5411438/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pezzuto et al., rapid anisotropic activation and ECG computation</a>; <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10717899/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ionic mechanisms of ischemic ST elevation</a>; <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4254140/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bundle-block modeling with invasive validation</a>. The last study illustrates the independent activation evidence that is absent from these source records.</p></div><h3>The Sandbox: events, then signals</h3><p>The Sandbox schedules atrial activation, conduction or block, and independent ventricular events before generating an ECG. A nonconducted P wave remains present without a triggered ventricular beat. Complete AV block uses an independent escape rhythm; AF can coexist with that block while atrial activity remains disorganized. PR-based block examples require organized atrial activity. One atrial rhythm and one bundle pattern are chosen at a time, and the model explains how compatible changes interact.</p><p>Hypertrophy controls change illustrative chamber-voltage contributions. Voltage and axis changes do not, by themselves, establish contraction strength, chamber dimensions or contractile dysfunction. The generated signals are teaching examples, not predictions of a patient’s ECG. The gray reference is a separate unmodified sinus model.</p><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Module 1 · Cardiac Voltage: from ions to EKG</b><p>The complete imported experience contains four chapters: The membrane, The wave, The lead and The whole heart. Its original lessons, interactions, microscopy gallery, human optical recordings, calculated 3D source model, methods and citations are retained. Its own “The science” and “Model notes &amp; sources” controls explain the distinct evidence behind each view.</p><p>The membrane and strand illustrations are reduced teaching models. The recorded human optical maps show processed, locally normalized fluorescence rather than calibrated membrane millivolts. The calculated four-chamber beat is calibrated to one measured lead-II ECG; the other calculated leads are exploratory and have separate fit limitations. The displayed modeled beat stops at 800 ms without compressing the retained source interval.</p><p><a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/WAVE_MODEL_NOTES.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Strand model notes ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/human/README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Human optical data and processing ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/FOUR_CHAMBER_METHODS.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Four-chamber methods ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/LEAD_II_CALIBRATION.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lead-II calibration and limits ↗</a>.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Module 1 · retained data, geometry and microscopy sources</b><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0231695\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Smirnov et al. 2020 ↗</a> supplies the human optical recordings, under CC0 1.0. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008851\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rodero et al. 2021 ↗</a> supplies the four-chamber anatomical source, under CC BY 4.0; the added left atrial appendage is explicitly inferred. The measured calibration reference is PTB Diagnostic ECG Database record patient104/s0306lre, under ODC-By 1.0. Atrial action-potential parameters derive from the credited Courtemanche–Ramirez–Nattel implementation. These are separate sources from the BodyParts3D reference anatomy and clinical library used in the remaining lessons.</p><p><a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/assets/microscopy/provenance.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microscopy image credits ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/four-chamber-geometry-attribution.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Geometry attribution ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/ptb-healthy-reference.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Measured ECG reference ↗</a> · <a href=\"./cardiac-voltage/data/heart-model-reproducible-source.zip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Retained scientific source package ↗</a>. Local font licenses, data metadata and the original source notices remain with the imported assets.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Bioelectric fields and lead systems · Malmivuo &amp; Plonsey</b><p><a href=\"https://bem.fi/book/08/08.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bioelectromagnetism, source modeling ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://bem.fi/book/15/15.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The 12-lead ECG system ↗</a>. In Modules 2, 3, 5 and 6, lead arrows remain fixed in patient coordinates. Solid positive halves, dashed negative halves and matching labels show polarity; labels keep their readable screen size during camera changes and spread apart when crowded. Individual selection and All 12 use the same axis display. Limb arrows show standard frontal directions; chest arrows are geometric guides toward the approximate chest electrodes. These are not calculated volume-conductor lead fields. Camera rotation changes the view, not the signal. MLII has no invented standard axis when its modified electrode geometry is unavailable.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>ECG standardization · AHA / ACCF / HRS</b><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2007.01.024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Technology and electrode placement (2007) ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2008.12.013\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">QRS conduction (2009) ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2008.12.014\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ST–T and QT interpretation (2009) ↗</a>. The lab teaches measurement principles, not a complete diagnostic or clinical decision system.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Modules 2, 3, 5 and 6 · BodyParts3D 4.0 reference anatomy</b><p>BodyParts3D, © The Database Center for Life Science licensed under <a href=\"https://dbarchive.biosciencedbc.jp/en/bodyparts3d/lic.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC Attribution 4.0 International ↗</a>. Heart mesh prepared by <a href=\"https://github.com/organui/heart-3d/tree/c72ce6654ff8e282942f97750d3104f55ef4fc3d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OrganUI ↗</a>. Ribs, cartilage, spine, cropped chest skin and the cranial inferior vena cava come from the same official dataset and retain the heart’s anatomical registration.</p><p>Three modeled collars bridge cut seams where the aortic segments meet and where the venae cavae meet the right atrium. Their ends follow the original surfaces during motion. These bridges are explicit modeling additions, not separately segmented anatomy. Skin and the inferior vena cava retain open crop edges; the lower caval crop is trimmed to an even transverse boundary. Materials, deformation, labels, conduction pathways and activation colors were added for this lab. Anatomy is not patient-specific; chest electrodes are illustrative placement anchors and starred limb markers denote remote connections.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Clinical library · 18 authentic recordings</b><p>The library contains <b>24 recorded examples: seventeen twelve-lead recordings and seven original two-channel excerpts</b>, lasting 6.6–14 seconds. Together they provide <b>373,400 calibrated channel samples</b>. Each waveform keeps its source sampling rate and recorded values, converted to mV without additional filtering, resampling, normalization or invented leads. The healthy PTB-XL recording remains the initial selection. The opening ideal rhythm and the Sandbox remain separately identified simulations.</p><p>A real tracing does not make the adjacent reference heart a reconstruction of that patient. Source annotations or explicitly approximate landmarks guide animation timing; spatial activation, recovery, highlighted territories and every contraction remain schematic. Source diagnoses and coexisting findings are retained without independent clinical re-adjudication.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>PTB-XL v1.0.3 · 14 recordings</b><p>Wagner et al. <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ptb-xl/1.0.3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PTB-XL / PhysioNet ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-020-0495-6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dataset paper ↗</a>. The original fourteen-record, ten-second, twelve-lead bundle uses the provided 100 Hz edition, under <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC BY 4.0 ↗</a>: 168,000 samples. All 168 PTB-XL channel checksums were verified against the source headers. Ten-millisecond sample spacing limits fine interval measurement in those original 100 Hz examples. Record 03155 (complete RBBB) is now displayed from its original 500 Hz edition, also used by the fit explorer; all 12 original channel checksums and the published file SHA-256 were verified. Both editions remain downloadable.</p><p>Source diagnoses, coexisting findings and signal-quality notes are retained. Complete-block record 00959 also carries incomplete RBBB and a low-confidence infarction label. The RVH example has the source’s normal-axis category (MID). Source axis categories come from report metadata; the lab does not measure a numerical patient axis. <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ptb-xl/1.0.3/ptbxl_database.csv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recording metadata ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ptb-xl/1.0.3/scp_statements.csv\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Annotation definitions ↗</a>.</p><p>QRS landmarks used for animation are approximate. Wenckebach P timing is visually estimated. Complete-block record 00959 has no reliable complete P-onset series, so its independent atrial clock is illustrative and guided by the source report. These are not validated interval measurements.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>Mobitz II with RBBB · MIT-BIH record 231</b><p>Contains information from George Moody and Roger Mark’s <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/mitdb/1.0.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database v1.0.0 / PhysioNet ↗</a>, under <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/mitdb/view-license/1.0.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ODC-By 1.0 ↗</a>. This excerpt keeps the first ten seconds at 360 Hz in the original <b>MLII and V1 channels</b>: 7,200 samples. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/51.932724\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Original database publication ↗</a>.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/mitdb/1.0.0/231.hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source header ↗</a> and <a href=\"https://physionet.org/physiobank/database/html/mitdbdir/records.htm#231\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official example directory ↗</a> identify Mobitz II with rate-related RBBB. Ten ventricular anchors retain the source beat annotations. Eleven P-onset estimates, including the nonconducted event near 5.735 s, were added for teaching synchronization. The 45 ms QRS-onset offset and reference QRS duration are illustrative. MLII is a modified ambulatory lead; its exact axis is omitted because the electrode coordinates are unavailable. No missing standard leads are synthesized.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>STEMI · ACS dataset, Figshare</b><p>Du et al. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.29925314\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACS ECG dataset ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-026-07278-0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dataset paper, Scientific Data (2026) ↗</a>. The downloadable dataset uses <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CC0 1.0 ↗</a>. Two full ten-second, twelve-lead recordings retain all 120,000 samples at 500 Hz.</p><p><b>ACS00004</b> has source STEMI and acute proximal LAD occlusion labels and is illustrated in the paper. <b>ACS18206</b> has source STEMI and acute proximal RCA occlusion labels. The displayed anterior and inferior pattern names are teaching interpretations of the recorded lead distribution and culprit-vessel context, rather than separately supplied source wall-location diagnoses.</p><p>The animation uses visually reviewed QRS extrema in lead I, with an illustrative 40 ms onset offset and prescribed atrial timing. These are not measured P/PR fiducials. Recorded noise and baseline variation remain. Selected raw archive entries passed CRC checks; inconsistent source header checksum fields are disclosed in the provenance and are not described as valid WFDB checksum verification.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>Overlapping STEMI regions · LUDB record 21</b><p>Contains information from Kalyakulina et al., <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ludb/1.0.1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lobachevsky University Electrocardiography Database v1.0.1 / PhysioNet ↗</a>, under <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ludb/view-license/1.0.1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ODC-By 1.0 ↗</a>. The 6.6-second excerpt retains source seconds 1.6–8.2 at 500 Hz, in all twelve leads: 39,600 samples. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2020.3029211\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Original LUDB publication ↗</a>.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/ludb/1.0.1/data/21.hea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source diagnosis ↗</a> includes anterior, lateral, septal and apical STEMI, left anterior hemiblock and left-axis deviation. The lateral teaching focus is part of that combined presentation. The crop includes eight fully annotated P/QRS complexes; original cardiologist lead-II onset, peak and end annotations guide timing, shifted by the excerpt offset. Spatial spread and contraction remain reference illustrations.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>Regional ST interpretation</b><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2008.12.016\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AHA/ACCF/HRS ischemia standards ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/ehjacc/article/13/1/55/7280662\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2023 ESC ACS guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://www.heartfoundation.org.au/for-professionals/acs-guideline?tab=2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Australian ACS guideline 2025 ↗</a>. These sources support the teaching discussion of regional and reciprocal changes. The warm region on the reference heart is an approximate anatomical guide. ST displacement does not quantify infarct size, contractile dysfunction or ejection fraction. The waveform, source diagnosis and explanatory model each retain their own evidentiary limits.</p></div>\n<div class=\"source-item\"><b>Mechanics: longitudinal shortening, torsion and regional deformation</b><p>Tagged-MRI research informs the kinds of motion shown: <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11153703/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">regional three-dimensional motion ↗</a>, <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10671594/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">myocardial deformation ↗</a>, and <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11545133/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ventricular mechanics ↗</a>. Actual moving references were reviewed: <a href=\"https://www.vhlab.umn.edu/atlas/cardiac-mri-tutorial/functional-assessment.shtml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">normal-human short-axis cine MRI · Visible Heart Lab ↗</a> and <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12880-017-0189-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Seemann et al., AV-plane displacement with long-axis cine movies ↗</a>. The videos show cavity narrowing, wall thickening and valve-plane motion. Model magnitudes remain illustrative; no ejection fraction or patient strain is inferred.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Conduction anatomy and the electrical cutaway</b><p><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-017-07694-8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stephenson et al., human conduction-system micro-CT (2017) ↗</a>. The bundle branches and fascicles supply distributed inner-wall muscle junctions. The apical fans and rising peripheral branches remain visible, with additional early septal delivery. Atrial routes show preferential spread through working myocardium and the Bachmann region. These are specialized cardiac muscle cells. The drawn tree is a reference teaching network, not segmented patient conduction tissue; it does not resolve every Purkinje branch, valve or microscopic fiber.</p><p><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1161/01.CIR.41.6.899\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Durrer et al., isolated human-heart mapping (1970) ↗</a> found several early endocardial activation sites, rather than one universal apex-first origin. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10154624/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choi et al., rabbit optical mapping (2023) ↗</a> demonstrated discrete Purkinje–muscle junctions and a short transmission delay. The delay in this visualization is an explicit teaching assumption, not a measured human or patient-specific constant. Fast insulated trunks can pass adjacent resting muscle.</p><p><a href=\"./data/pathology-fits/PATHWAY_MUSCLE_SYNCHRONIZATION.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shared activation method and synchronization checks ↗</a>. The conducting pulse, muscle voltage, selected cellular trace and activation map use shared arrival data. The recorded clinical waveforms and native CT fitted-source examples retain their original samples and calculations.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Clinical mechanisms</b><p><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11095842/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2023 AF guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000000628\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2018 bradycardia and conduction guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2008.12.015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AHA/ACCF/HRS chamber-hypertrophy standards ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/joa3.12872\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2023 physiologic pacing guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26900418/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RBBB mechanics study ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4807630/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PVC mechanics measured by real-time CMR ↗</a>.</p><p>These sources inform the distinctions between atrial activity, AV conduction, ventricular activation and later contraction. The teaching model preserves atrial events during dropped beats, separates atrial and escape clocks in complete block, and delays regional ventricular activation in bundle branch block. ECG morphology does not measure contraction force or identify an exact escape site or ectopic focus here.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Adult measurement reference</b><p>The AHA ECG standards linked above guide PR, QRS and QTc conventions; <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2008.12.015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Part V ↗</a> informs atrial criteria. HR 60–100 bpm and RR 600–1000 ms describe conventional resting adult sinus rhythm. P duration is usually &lt;120 ms; usual limb-lead P amplitude ≤0.25 mV; PR 120–200 ms; QRS often 70–110 ms. QT is rate dependent. QTc prolongation thresholds of 450 ms for men and 460 ms for women are practical conventions, not a universal diagnosis. No universal normal duration is assigned to ST or T.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Local action potentials · seven synchronized sampling sites</b><p>The Module 2 rows show illustrative intracellular-minus-extracellular cell voltages, not clinical ECG recordings. They share the heart clock, while the aligned surface ECG remains a separate educational electrode-signal model. Specialized SA/AV/His/Purkinje curves retain their prescribed literature-informed functions. Atrial and ventricular working-myocyte rows now evaluate the same registered field and imported action-potential templates as the 3D surface. The atrial template comes from the Courtemanche human atrial model; the ventricular recovery shape uses pooled human optical recordings with assumed absolute voltage and intrinsic upstroke. No ionic differential-equation solver is run in the browser. Selecting a row marks one registered site in the reference anatomy. Pathway pulses and cellular upstrokes share timing constants; in the reference beat the sampled His site activates 43 ms before QRS onset. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1378/chest.64.1.55\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dhingra et al. 1973</a> and <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1126224/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lee &amp; Lien 1975</a> provide primary human intracardiac timing evidence. His electrograms are extracellular recordings, distinct from the illustrative local cell-voltage trace.</p><p>Primary sources: <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17823213/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Verkerk 2007, human sinoatrial patch-clamp recordings</a>; <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28185290/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fabbri 2017, human sinus-node model</a>; <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9688927/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Courtemanche 1998, human atrial model</a>; <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2764063/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inada 2009, rabbit AV-node model</a>; <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19414454/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stewart 2009, Purkinje-cell model</a>; <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16565318/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ten Tusscher &amp; Panfilov 2006, human ventricular model</a>. Rabbit AV evidence and the representative His curve are explicitly distinguished from human cellular recordings. Latent AV/Purkinje escape automaticity and detailed rate adaptation are outside this normal-heart panel.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Recovery maps · activation time + local action-potential duration</b><p>Recovery is a local membrane process. The field couples ventricular activation to the visible pathway model, while retaining the recovery-time pattern transferred from the Module 1 CT model. Local AP duration adjusts to the new activation time, with a separate outward wall delay and timing adaptation to the displayed ECG. It has transmural, apicobasal and RV/LV differences, with shorter epicardial action potentials. A later-activated cell can recover sooner, but a whole-heart reverse activation sequence is not a universal human rule. The selected example has earlier apical recovery; human maps can differ with age, tissue conditions and activation sequence. No recovery pulse is sent backward through the conduction fibers.</p><p><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16606830/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ramanathan et al. 2006</a> used noninvasive mapping in healthy adults and linked recovery strongly to local duration. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26209251/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Boukens et al. 2015</a> mapped human donor LV wedges: shorter outer-wall action potentials substantially offset their later activation. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27737802/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Opthof et al. 2017</a> measured different recovery gradients along multiple axes in intact isolated human hearts. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1113/JP288356\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sobota et al., online 2025</a> reported human apicobasal variability and an age association. These studies inform interpretation. The CT activation/APD atlas and pooled optical waveform come from separate reference sources; no participant-specific recovery map was recovered from the displayed teaching ECG.</p><p>The LV endocardial/epicardial probes use actual registered wall vertices and the same timing function as the 3D colors. Their displayed activation and 90% recovery times are generated by the shared field at the selected rate. They are model outputs, not measured patient cell timings. The T wave reflects combined spatial recovery differences and cannot be reduced to either one wall gradient or the seven displayed voltages.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>New fast rhythms · authentic MIT-BIH and VFDB recordings</b><p>The <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/mitdb/1.0.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database ↗</a> provides AF with rapid ventricular response (record 202), atrial flutter (222) and supraventricular tachyarrhythmia (209). The ten-second excerpts retain the two source channels at 360 Hz. Source beat positions give mean ventricular rates of approximately 124, 102 and 160/min, respectively. RVR is the interpretation of this AF excerpt’s measured fast ventricular response; it is not a separate source diagnostic code.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/vfdb/1.0.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MIT-BIH Malignant Ventricular Ectopy Database ↗</a> supplies VT (421) and VF (422), with two source ECG channels at 250 Hz. Its annotations identify rhythm windows, not individual beats. Forty reviewed waveform maxima provide approximate VT synchronization and an estimated rate near 246/min; the source does not adjudicate the VT subtype or circuit. No discrete QRS events or rate are assigned to VF. Original calibration, baseline variation and noise remain visible.</p><p>These records are used under the Open Data Commons Attribution License v1.0. ECG1/ECG2 distinguish two channels whose original labels are both ECG; no standard lead identity or 3D axis is invented. <a href=\"./data/clinical-tachyarrhythmias-README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Crops, attribution and qualifications ↗</a> · <a href=\"./data/clinical-tachyarrhythmias-provenance.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Import evidence ↗</a>.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Torsades de pointes · a published prolonged-QT episode</b><p>Two original 250 Hz channels from <a href=\"https://physionet.org/content/sddb/1.0.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sudden Cardiac Death Holter Database record 47 ↗</a> are shown for source elapsed time 22390–22404 s. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jelectrocard.2006.04.011\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lerma et al., 2007 ↗</a> classify the episode as TdP and document prolonged preceding QTc; their Figure 2 identifies the relevant segment. This is the digital source waveform, not a digitized figure. The paper is linked as evidence and is not redistributed.</p><p>The first eight seconds contain preceding complex ventricular activity; approximately the final six seconds correspond to the source-identified TdP segment. The alignment is graphical, with about one second uncertainty. No exact onset, beat fiducials, termination or subsequent VF transition is claimed. The prolonged-QT context comes from the study, not a measurement made on the tachycardia crop. Motion timing is explicitly schematic, and no heart rate or beat-jump markers are fabricated. ODC-By 1.0. <a href=\"./data/clinical-torsades-README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Source correspondence and limitations ↗</a> · <a href=\"./data/clinical-torsades-provenance.json\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Calibration and provenance ↗</a>.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Tachyarrhythmia mechanisms · reference illustrations</b><p>AF shows disorganized atrial activity with irregular ventricular responses. Flutter shows an explicitly schematic typical right-atrial loop; its source label does not establish the patient’s circuit. SVTA retains a supraventricular category without inventing AVNRT, AVRT or atrial timing. VT uses a generic ventricular-origin activation pattern. TdP varies spatial activation without physically rotating the heart. VF shows changing local electrical activity and quivering without a coordinated ejection squeeze. Atrial activity is marked unavailable wherever its timing is unmeasured.</p><p><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11095842/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2023 AF guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/41/5/655/5556821\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2019 ESC supraventricular tachycardia guideline ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6595359/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2019 ventricular arrhythmia consensus ↗</a> · <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3057430/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AHA/ACCF scientific statement on TdP ↗</a>. These explain mechanisms; they do not supply this reference model with patient-specific activation maps, contraction measurements or hemodynamics.</p></div><div class=\"source-item\"><b>Modules 2–6 rendering · Three.js</b><p>Three.js r180, MIT license. All geometry, renderer code and recording files are served with this site. Camera controls change the view, not the physiological signal.</p></div><p style=\"margin-top:20px\">EKG and ECG refer to the same recording. The lab uses both terms. Normal ranges here are teaching guides for adults and do not replace clinical context.</p>";
